-- name: Files touched outside any declared plan
-- class: Consistency
-- metric: alignment_collect_d1
-- action: declare the touched files in a slice or plan, or confirm the out-of-plan work was deliberate scope
-- severity: WARN
-- guardrail: BOTH SIDES OF THIS DISAGREEMENT LIVE IN alignment-collect's OWN d1 RECORD -- spec docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-30-loopsmith-insight-data-platform-design.md:529's own parenthetical names d1 files_changed_outside_any_plan directly; alignment-collect.sh's own printf (lines 445-446) emits commits_with_source and files_changed_outside_any_plan together in one object, so no join is needed or possible. ROUND-2 POST-PR-REVIEW BLOCKING FIX (PR #203, second pass), the identical class of defect as the sibling consistency_verify_no_test_touched.sql rule, applied here: the prior round's json_type(raw_payload,'$.dimensions.d1')='OBJECT' guard protected only the d1 CONTAINER's own shape, never a SIBLING SCALAR field read out of a container that genuinely IS an object -- commits_with_source shaped as a nested JSON object crashed a bare CAST(... AS INTEGER) with DuckDB's ConversionException (COALESCE only catches a NULL result, never an exception), reproduced live against the reviewer's own repro payload, aborting evaluate_rule entirely and hiding every well-formed row sharing that scan. FIXED IN TWO LAYERS, matching the sibling rule exactly. LAYER 1, UNCONDITIONAL CRASH-SAFETY: commits_with_source and files_changed_outside_any_plan are now extracted with TRY_CAST, never bare CAST -- TRY_CAST returns SQL NULL for ANY conversion failure instead of raising, confirmed live against every malformed shape tried (object, array, string, bool, int cross-casts). CONFIRMED LIVE, POST-PR-REVIEW, THAT A json_type GUARD ALONE DOES NOT RELIABLY PREVENT THIS CLASS OF CRASH (a minimal reproduction with the guard present as an AND operand still raised, proving DuckDB does not always short-circuit AND before evaluating a later expression on the same field) -- TRY_CAST is the mechanism this rule relies on for crash-safety, not a redundant extra. LAYER 2, PER-FIELD json_type GUARDS, IN BOTH POPULATION AND EVIDENCE, FOR A DIFFERENT REASON THAN CRASH-PREVENTION -- TRY_CAST IS PERMISSIVE: for files_changed_outside_any_plan specifically, TRY_CAST(... AS VARCHAR[]) NEVER coerces a non-array (confirmed live: object/string/int/bool all TRY_CAST to NULL, never to a spurious single-element or split array), so there is NO coercion-leak risk for this field, and the guard's role in evidence is purely the false-PASS defense round 1 already established (the nested "well-formed d1, wrong-shaped files_changed_outside_any_plan sub-field" case, still reproducible against '{"dimensions":{"d1":{"commits_with_source":5,"files_changed_outside_any_plan":"scratch.py,notes.md"}}}'). commits_with_source IS DIFFERENT: TRY_CAST(... AS INTEGER) DOES coerce a numeric STRING ("5") or a JSON BOOLEAN into a real integer, confirmed live -- so, unlike files_changed_outside_any_plan, commits_with_source's OWN guard genuinely prevents a coercion-leak, mutation-tested (test_a_coercible_commits_with_source_string_does_not_leak_into_evidence: with only the evidence-side commits_with_source guard removed, a mixed store -- one well-formed pack, one with commits_with_source: "5" -- returned population=1 correctly but a SECOND, wrongly-coerced evidence row). commits_with_source's OWN GUARD IS DELIBERATELY SOFTER THAN files_changed_outside_any_plan's -- `json_type(...) IS NULL OR json_type(...) IN ('UBIGINT','BIGINT')`, NOT a bare IN(...): commits_with_source is a SECONDARY, existence-only gate here (this rule's own measured dimension is files_changed_outside_any_plan), and a well-formed files_changed_outside_any_plan array with NO commits_with_source key at all is the analogous, explicitly OUT-OF-SCOPE case the sibling rule's own review named (pinned here by test_a_wellformed_files_changed_array_with_no_commits_with_source_at_all_is_pass) -- the IS NULL branch preserves PASS for a MISSING commits_with_source while the second branch still excludes a PRESENT-but-wrong-typed one (ABSENT), proven by test_commits_with_source_shaped_as_an_object_is_absent_not_a_crash. THE EVIDENCE-SIDE COPY OF THE files_changed_outside_any_plan ARRAY GUARD WAS DELETED, RE-DERIVED, NOT KEPT AS UNEXPLAINED DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH -- mutation-tested: with json_array_length() itself confirmed live to return 0 (never raise, never miscount) for EVERY non-array JSON type (object, string, int, bool all return 0, not a spurious nonzero count), and TRY_CAST(... AS VARCHAR[]) confirmed to have no coercion path at all, removing the evidence-side copy of this guard (population's own copy left intact) changed the output of every test in this file not at all -- so it was deleted from the evidence WHERE rather than kept as redundant-but-unexplained cruft; population's OWN copy remains, fully load-bearing (mutation-confirmed: its removal breaks five different tests). THE TOP-LEVEL d1='OBJECT' CONTAINER CHECK IS GONE, NOT MERELY UNUSED -- RE-DERIVED, NOT ASSUMED, mirroring the sibling rule's own note: with the per-field guards above, a d1 that is itself null/array/scalar makes the dotted path into commits_with_source/files_changed_outside_any_plan resolve to SQL NULL (json path traversal through a non-object parent, confirmed live to never raise), which already fails the per-field guards on its own -- confirmed by mutation (reverting the container check to the round-1 shape while the per-field guards stayed caught nothing), so it was deleted. THE EVIDENCE ARRAY COLUMN IS A DIRECT TRY_CAST(... AS VARCHAR[]) OF THE JSON ARRAY -- proven live to return a native Python list through the DB-API with no UNNEST needed (Design decision 4), matching coverage_gate_absent.sql's/coverage_degraded_collector.sql's own array-evidence precedent for degraded_collector/degraded_adapter. REQUIRES commits_with_source > 0 (an existence check, not a magnitude) SO A PACK WITH NO REAL SOURCE ACTIVITY NEVER FIRES ON A STALE OR EMPTY ARRAY -- COALESCE(json_array_length(...), 0) > 0 mirrors 30.sql's own COALESCE(len(...), 0) array-emptiness idiom, adapted to json_array_length because the value here is a JSON value extracted from raw_payload, not yet a native VARCHAR[] column. files_outside_plan_confidence IS CARRIED THROUGH AS AN HONEST, NON-GATING, INFORMATIONAL COLUMN, READ VIA json_extract_string, WHICH NEVER RAISES ON ANY JSON TYPE (confirmed live: it safely stringifies an object, array, number, or bool alike, returning SQL NULL only for a JSON null literal) -- no guard is needed for this specific column for that reason; alignment-collect.sh itself always emits "low" here today (its own printf line 445), never varying per pack, so this rule's WHERE clause never reads or gates on it either way (Design decision 6), the same passthrough posture coverage_gate_absent.sql already established for degraded_collector/degraded_adapter. WHY d1-SOURCED RATHER THAN A fact_slice JOIN -- fact_slice has a real writer (artifact_reader.py's write_slices) but no join key to any commit or collector-pack row: a slice's own files[] is a plan-time declaration with no timestamp or commit-sha correlation to d1's observed per-commit file list, and this repo has no .slices.json at all today; alignment-collect.sh already performs the file-vs-plan correlation this rule needs (its own found_in_plan logic, lines ~323-337), so this rule reads that already-computed result rather than re-deriving it against a disconnected table (Design decision 3's rejected alternative). MALFORMED-PAYLOAD DISCLOSURE, RE-SCOPED AGAIN THIS ROUND: SYNTACTICALLY INVALID JSON (raw_payload is non-NULL text that will not parse as JSON at all) still makes every json_extract/json_type call in this rule's population and evidence queries raise DuckDB's InvalidInputException -- TRY_CAST does not protect against this, because json_extract/json_type themselves raise while parsing the raw text, before any CAST/TRY_CAST ever runs; this fails LOUD, not silent, so it is not spec:534's forbidden collapse, and it is the identical, unguarded pattern every one of the six already-shipped rules (and metrics 5/20/24/41) shares against the same raw_payload column -- widening scope to guard against syntactically-invalid JSON across the catalog is out of this story's scope (see .sdlc/plans/120.md Risks). VALID JSON OF THE WRONG SHAPE, AT ANY FIELD THIS RULE READS, now renders ABSENT (or, for a missing-but-not-malformed commits_with_source specifically, the out-of-scope PASS), never a crash and never a silent false PASS. ORDER BY'S TOTAL-ORDER CLAIM, CORRECTED (LOW finding, post-PR-review round 2): `rowid` is deterministic WITHIN A SINGLE QUERY EXECUTION against an append-only table -- it is NOT a persistent row identity across executions once any DELETE has occurred (a delete followed by a CHECKPOINT can renumber a surviving row's own rowid, DuckDB's own documented physical-storage-position semantics, verified by an independent reviewer) -- no DELETE FROM fact_collector_pack exists under insight/ today, so this ordering guarantee holds in practice, but the claim here is scoped to exactly that: one query's own result-set ordering is deterministic, not a durable identity fact_collector_pack itself carries (it has no primary key, and collected_ts alone is not unique either) -- the same disclosed limitation coverage_degraded_collector.sql's own ORDER BY carries, closed here for THIS rule's own evidence rows only. A NOTE ON json_type OF A JSON NULL LITERAL, VERIFIED LIVE AGAIN THIS ROUND, BYTE-FOR-BYTE, AGAINST THE EXACT TWO-ARGUMENT FORM THIS RULE USES (`json_type(raw_payload, path)`, duckdb 1.4.5): it returns the four-character STRING 'NULL' (Python `str`, `IS NULL` evaluates to FALSE for it), distinguishable from a genuinely missing key, which returns actual SQL NULL (Python `None`, `IS NULL` evaluates to TRUE) -- reconfirmed directly before writing this sentence; this does not change any guard's own behavior (both outcomes correctly fail every `= 'X'`/`IN (...)` comparison here either way), but the specific factual claim is restated here, with its own verification command, precisely because a prior review round asserted the opposite of what this session's own live check shows.
-- population: SELECT count(*) FROM fact_collector_pack WHERE schema = 'alignment-collect/v1' AND (json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') IS NULL OR json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') IN ('UBIGINT', 'BIGINT')) AND json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan') = 'ARRAY'
SELECT
    project_id,
    collected_ts,
    TRY_CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') AS INTEGER) AS commits_with_source,
    TRY_CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan') AS VARCHAR[]) AS files_changed_outside_any_plan,
    json_extract_string(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_outside_plan_confidence') AS files_outside_plan_confidence
FROM fact_collector_pack
WHERE schema = 'alignment-collect/v1'
  AND (json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') IS NULL
       OR json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') IN ('UBIGINT', 'BIGINT'))
  AND COALESCE(TRY_CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') AS INTEGER), 0) > 0
  AND COALESCE(json_array_length(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan')), 0) > 0
ORDER BY project_id, collected_ts, rowid
